  "settingsWordOrderGuide": "Word order guide",
  "settingsWordOrderGuideSubtitle": "How Thai word order works",
  "wordOrderLearnMore": "More on word order",
  "wordOrderGuideTitle": "Thai word order guide",
  "wordOrderGuideHeading": "Thai word order basics",
  "wordOrderGuideIntro": "Stuck? Thai usually puts the topic first, verbs before objects, and describing words after their nouns.",
  "wordOrderRule1Title": "Put the topic first",
  "wordOrderRule1Body": "Thai often names what you are talking about first, then says something about it. Places and nouns marked with นี้/นั้น (this/that) often serve as topics.",
  "wordOrderRule2Title": "Specific things can be topics",
  "wordOrderRule2Body": "Something already mentioned and marked with นี้/นั้น can go at the start, and the rest of the sentence says something about it.",
  "wordOrderRule3Title": "After the topic: who → does → what",
  "wordOrderRule3Body": "The verb comes before its object, as in English.",
  "wordOrderRule4Title": "Descriptions follow nouns",
  "wordOrderRule4Body": "Adjectives follow the noun. The owner also comes after the thing owned.",
  "wordOrderRule5Title": "Noun → number → classifier → this",
  "wordOrderRule5Body": "Numbers follow the noun, together with a classifier (a counting word). \"This\" comes after that.",
  "wordOrderRule6Title": "ไม่ (not) goes before what it negates",
  "wordOrderRule6Body": "ไม่ goes right before what it negates.",
  "wordOrderRule7Title": "Question words stay in place",
  "wordOrderRule7Body": "Question words stay where the answer goes, though ทำไม (why) often comes first. For yes/no questions, add ไหม after the predicate (polite endings go after it).",
  "wordOrderRule8Title": "Words like จะ and แล้ว",
  "wordOrderRule8Body": "จะ (will), กำลัง (be doing) and เคย (have ever) go before the verb. แล้ว (already) goes after the verb and its object.",
  "wordOrderRule9Title": "Time: first/last · place: after verb",
  "wordOrderRule9Body": "Time expressions go first or last. Places usually follow the verb.",
  "wordOrderRule10Title": "Polite endings come last",
  "wordOrderRule10Body": "Men use ครับ. Women usually use ค่ะ, and คะ in questions. They go after ไหม, แล้ว and sentence-final words like นะ, เลย and ด้วย. After นะ, women use คะ (นะคะ).",
  "wordOrderTipsTitle": "Word-order tips",
  "wordOrderTipsBody": "If a place or \"this ...\" is the topic of the whole sentence, put it first\nFind the action or state word. \"Who\" goes before it; \"what\" and \"where\" go after it\nDescribing words, numbers, นี้ and มาก go after what they describe\nกับ, ใน etc. go before the noun; อยาก and ต้อง go before the verb\nไหม, แล้ว, นะ and ครับ/ค่ะ/คะ go near the end",
  "wordOrderEx1aGloss": "tour|this|people|many",
  "wordOrderEx1aMeaning": "This tour is crowded",
  "wordOrderEx1bGloss": "airport|people|many",
  "wordOrderEx1bMeaning": "The airport is crowded",
  "wordOrderRule1Note": "Thai usually doesn't repeat a topic with a pronoun like \"they\".",
  "wordOrderEx2aGloss": "report|this|tomorrow|use|can|?",
  "wordOrderEx2aMeaning": "Can we use this report tomorrow?",
  "wordOrderEx3aGloss": "I|eat|rice",
  "wordOrderEx3aMeaning": "I eat rice",
  "wordOrderRule3Note": "Obvious \"who\" and \"what\" are often omitted.",
  "wordOrderEx4aGloss": "house|big",
  "wordOrderEx4aMeaning": "a big house",
  "wordOrderEx4bGloss": "house|of|me",
  "wordOrderEx4bMeaning": "my house",
  "wordOrderRule4Note": "บ้านใหญ่ can also mean \"The house is big\": adjectives work as predicates without เป็น. ของ can be left out.",
  "wordOrderEx5aGloss": "cat|two|[classifier]",
  "wordOrderEx5aMeaning": "two cats",
  "wordOrderEx5bGloss": "cat|two|[classifier]|this",
  "wordOrderEx5bMeaning": "these two cats",
  "wordOrderEx6aGloss": "I|not|eat|spicy",
  "wordOrderEx6aMeaning": "I don't eat spicy food",
  "wordOrderEx6bGloss": "I|didn't|go",
  "wordOrderEx6bMeaning": "I didn't go",
  "wordOrderEx6cGloss": "go|can't",
  "wordOrderEx6cMeaning": "I can't go",
  "wordOrderRule6Note": "ไม่ได้ + verb often means \"didn't\" or \"am not doing\". In \"can't\", ไม่ได้ comes after the verb.",
  "wordOrderEx7aGloss": "you|eat|what",
  "wordOrderEx7aMeaning": "What do you eat?",
  "wordOrderEx7bGloss": "go|where",
  "wordOrderEx7bMeaning": "Where are you going?",
  "wordOrderEx7cGloss": "eat|?",
  "wordOrderEx7cMeaning": "Will you eat?",
  "wordOrderEx8aGloss": "I|will|go",
  "wordOrderEx8aMeaning": "I will go",
  "wordOrderEx8bGloss": "eat|rice|already",
  "wordOrderEx8bMeaning": "I've already eaten",
  "wordOrderEx9aGloss": "tomorrow|I|will|go|Chiang Mai",
  "wordOrderEx9aMeaning": "Tomorrow I'll go to Chiang Mai",
  "wordOrderEx9bGloss": "I|work|at|Bangkok",
  "wordOrderEx9bMeaning": "I work in Bangkok",
  "wordOrderEx10aGloss": "eat|?|polite (female)",
  "wordOrderEx10aMeaning": "Will you eat?",
  "wordOrderEx10bGloss": "eat|already|polite (female)",
  "wordOrderEx10bMeaning": "I've already eaten",
  "wordOrderRule5Note": "หนึ่ง after the classifier, as in แมวตัวหนึ่ง, means \"a certain cat\".",
  "wordOrderEx10cGloss": "tasty|[softener]|polite (female)",
  "wordOrderEx10cMeaning": "It's tasty, isn't it?",
  "wordOrderRule11Title": "Clauses: noun → ที่ → description",
  "wordOrderRule11Body": "When a clause describes a noun (\"the shop I like\"), it still comes after: usually put ที่ after the noun, then the clause.",
  "wordOrderEx11aGloss": "shop|that|I|like",
  "wordOrderEx11aMeaning": "the shop I like",
  "wordOrderRule12Title": "Prepositions come before the noun",
  "wordOrderRule12Body": "กับ (with), ใน (in), บน (on), ให้ (to/for) and ที่ (at) go before the noun.",
  "wordOrderEx12aGloss": "I|go|with|friend",
  "wordOrderEx12aMeaning": "I'm going with a friend",
  "wordOrderEx12bGloss": "cat|be|on|table",
  "wordOrderEx12bMeaning": "The cat is on the table",
  "wordOrderRule13Title": "\"Very\" and \"a little\" come after",
  "wordOrderRule13Body": "มาก (very), จริงๆ (really) and นิดหน่อย (a little) follow the adjective or verb.",
  "wordOrderEx13aGloss": "food|this|tasty|very",
  "wordOrderEx13aMeaning": "This food is very tasty",
  "wordOrderRule14Title": "Comparing: adjective → กว่า → other",
  "wordOrderRule14Body": "\"A is more ... than B\" is A + adjective + กว่า + B. For \"the most ...\", add ที่สุด after the adjective.",
  "wordOrderEx14aGloss": "today|hot|more than|yesterday",
  "wordOrderEx14aMeaning": "Today is hotter than yesterday",
  "wordOrderEx14bGloss": "thing|this|good|most",
  "wordOrderEx14bMeaning": "This one is the best",
  "wordOrderRule15Title": "\"Want\" before the verb, \"can\" after",
  "wordOrderRule15Body": "อยาก (want to), ต้อง (must) and ควร (should) go before the verb. ได้ (can) goes after the verb and its object.",
  "wordOrderEx15aGloss": "I|want to|eat|som tam",
  "wordOrderEx15aMeaning": "I want to eat som tam",
  "wordOrderEx15bGloss": "I|speak|Thai|can",
  "wordOrderEx15bMeaning": "I can speak Thai",
  "wordOrderRule16Title": "Verbs line up in a row",
  "wordOrderRule16Body": "Thai strings verbs together in the order things happen: ไป (go) + ซื้อ (buy) means \"go shopping\". ไป/มา meaning \"away from\" or \"toward the speaker\" come at the end.",
  "wordOrderEx16aGloss": "I|go|buy|things",
  "wordOrderEx16aMeaning": "I'm going shopping",
  "wordOrderEx16bGloss": "take|water|come",
  "wordOrderEx16bMeaning": "Bring some water",
  "pronGuideTitle": "Thai pronunciation guide",
  "pronGuideHeading": "Key points of Thai pronunciation",
  "pronGuideIntro": "The app's readings are romanization with a few extra symbols. Learn the sounds behind them here. Tones are covered in the tone guide.",
  "pronSymbolsTitle": "Reading symbols",
  "pronSymbolsBody": "Only the symbols that differ from plain romanization.",
  "pronSymbolsDescs": "ɛ is an open \"e\" as in \"bed\"; ɔ is an open \"o\" as in \"law\"|\"u\" said with spread lips, not rounded|A relaxed sound like the \"u\" in \"fur\", without the r|A doubled vowel is long|k has no puff of air and often sounds close to \"g\"; kh has a strong puff|With h, a strong puff of air. t and p have none|\"ng\" as in \"sing\". It can start a word|Tones: no mark is mid, à low, â falling, á high, ǎ rising",
  "pronAspTitle": "With or without a puff of air",
  "pronAspBody": "kh, th, ph and ch have a strong puff of air; k, t, p and j have none, like the p in \"spin\" or the t in \"stop\". In Thai this difference changes the word.",
  "pronAspMeanings": "chicken|egg|eye|to apply|to throw|to take along",
  "pronAspNote": "Say them with your hand in front of your mouth: you should feel air on kh, th and ph, but not on k, t and p.",
  "pronLengthTitle": "Long and short vowels",
  "pronLengthBody": "A doubled vowel such as aa or ii is long. Length alone can change the word.",
  "pronLengthMeanings": "to enter|rice|he/she|white",
  "pronVowelTitle": "Telling similar vowels apart",
  "pronVowelBody": "e/ɛ, o/ɔ and u/ʉ are different vowels. Open your mouth wide for ɛ and ɔ, and keep your lips spread for ʉ.",
  "pronVowelMeanings": "child|sunshine|table|room|to look|hand|money|to walk",
  "pronFinalTitle": "Final k, t, p are not released",
  "pronFinalBody": "At the end of a word, k, t and p are stopped without releasing air. Also keep n and ng apart.",
  "pronFinalMeanings": "bird|car|with|plate|faded",
  "pronFinalNote": "In Thai script, final ด, ส, ช etc. sound like t, and final บ, พ etc. like p.",
  "pronCasualTitle": "Casual speech",
  "pronCasualBody": "In conversation, words often aren't said as written: r becomes l, the second consonant of a cluster drops, or the tone changes.",
  "pronCasualMeanings": "to love|polite (male)|question particle|I (female)",
  "pronCasualNote": "These forms help with listening. When speaking, the written reading is fine.",
